WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:06.120
This tiny heart is about to&nbsp;
beat for the very first time...

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:14.760
In this moment, a cluster of developing cells&nbsp;
begins a rhythmic pulse that will last a lifetime.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:21.174
But why now? What triggers the heart to start&nbsp;
beating at this precise moment?

00:00:21.174 --> 00:00:25.960
And how do all the cells start to contract in synchrony?

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.291
For each heart there's only one chance to catch that very first beat

00:00:30.291 --> 00:00:36.302
so scientists&nbsp;have been using microscopes, fluorescent proteins, arrays of zebrafish embryos

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:41.140
and a lot of&nbsp;patience to explore this pivotal moment...

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.466
These are zebrafish embryos&nbsp;
each less than one millimeter long.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.009 --> 00:01:01.680
Their hearts start beating only 20 hours&nbsp;
after fertilization, well before the growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:05.595
organism actually needs its heart functions.

00:01:05.595 --> 00:01:11.340
To visualize the heartbeats, the researchers used fluorescent proteins which glow&nbsp;in response to their surroundings.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.000
Here the pink fluorescent proteins represent a&nbsp;
voltage spike which causes a spike of Calcium&nbsp;ions,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:23.340
seen here in green. And it's this calcium&nbsp;
spike which causes the cells to contract.&nbsp;

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:30.060
In the adult fish, the beating of the heart is&nbsp;
controlled by a pacemaker region, a group of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:34.281
cells which control the speed of contractions in&nbsp;
the rest of the organ.

00:01:34.281 --> 00:01:39.602
But for the first heartbeats the researchers found no clear pacemakers;

00:01:39.602 --> 00:01:43.486
different&nbsp;embryos had different cells that started beating first

00:01:43.486 --> 00:01:47.838
and they triggered the cells around them&nbsp;in a wave-like pattern.

00:01:47.838 --> 00:01:53.820
In this experiment the cells in the bottom left of this image start&nbsp;the pulse which travels across the heart.

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.213
But when researchers silenced those cells,

00:01:58.213 --> 00:02:03.607
this&nbsp;region of cells takes over 
as the starting point of each pulse.

00:02:03.607 --> 00:02:08.276
So what starts the process? What&nbsp;
kicks off the chain reaction?

00:02:08.276 --> 00:02:12.157
The answer is: perhaps nothing in particular.

00:02:12.157 --> 00:02:19.653
It seems that as the&nbsp;cells mature in the developing embryo they grow gradually more and more sensitive to electrical&nbsp;
signals.

00:02:19.653 --> 00:02:24.915
After a while they become so sensitive to natural electrical noise in the tissue around them

00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:27.780
that some of the cells start activating at random.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:33.420
This activates the cells next to them and the&nbsp;
ones next to those and so on until a pulse spreads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.959
across the heart for the first time.

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:40.320
Gradually,&nbsp;eventually, a steady rhythm is established.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:47.952
The team think that this is how those very first&nbsp;
heartbeats originate - in quite a haphazard way.

00:02:47.952 --> 00:02:51.421
Very different to the tightly controlled rhythm of&nbsp;
the adult heart.

00:02:53.001 --> 00:02:57.066
Understanding this key point in development 
could help researchers study

00:02:57.066 --> 00:03:01.037
what goes&nbsp;wrong in embryos with congenital heart conditions.

00:03:01.037 --> 00:03:06.342
Or it could be useful for engineering stem&nbsp;cells that can be implanted into a damaged heart

00:03:06.342 --> 00:03:10.200
and which need to sync up with the&nbsp;
rhythm of the patient's own heart tissues.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:17.640
The heart works continuously during&nbsp;
its lifetime, through adrenaline rushes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:20.793
and exhaustion until our final moments... 

00:03:20.793 --> 00:03:24.601
but it&nbsp;couldn't happen without that very first heartbeat.

